% TRANSFORMATION TpppAspectNeutralization
< m1: PREPPFORMULA{PREPPFORMULArec1}[mu1]
>

< m : PREPPFORMULA{PREPPFORMULArec2}[mu1]
>

      COMP
         <
          C1: PREPPFORMULArec1.aspecto = imperfectivo
          A1: PREPPFORMULArec2 := PREPPFORMULArec1;
              PREPPFORMULArec2.aspecto := omegaaspecto;
         >

      DECOMP
         <
          C1: PREPPFORMULArec2.aspecto = omegaaspecto
          A1: PREPPFORMULArec1 := PREPPFORMULArec2;
              PREPPFORMULArec1.aspecto := imperfectivo;
         >

&

\end{verbatim}
\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] TPPPAspectNeutralization
\item[* relevant examples:] 
\item[* language:]  Spanish
\item[* kind of rule:] M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] PREPPFORMULAtoPREPPPROP
\item[* class:] Finaltransformations
\item[* date of creation:] 15-MAR-1989
\item[* author:] Lisette Appelo, Margreet Sanders
\item[* copied from:] Raspectneutralisation Spanish
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] This rule is for efficiency of the surface parser: in analysis,
this transformation performs a function of the surface parser and 
sets the attribute `aspect' off the PREPPFORMULA to `imperfective' 
(no perfective aspect of XPPROP is
allowed; see RpppAspectImperf), so that a real Aspect rule can reduce it to 
omega-aspect again. All this is needed to achieve isomorphy with the sentence 
grammar, where Aspect is an obligatory rule class.
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals) in contrast to 
the ADJAspect rules, the PREPP is not given any value for `aspect', since there 
does not seem any need to do so (temporal adverbials are placed directly under 
the PPPROP, not under the PP).
